+    /**
+     * Whether a gap wider than {@link #GAP_CAP_TW} may be SPENT as {@code w:before} instead of
+     * anchoring the line that follows it.
+     *
+     * <p>THE GAP IS STILL A POSITION, AND {@code w:before} IS A CONSTRUCT THAT SAYS SO. The cap
+     * exists because a gap wider than the page can afford pushes everything under it down the
+     * sheet (see {@link #GAP_CAP_TW}); an anchored text box was how the writer held the line at
+     * its own y without paying that. The box is not free either: Google Docs - the renderer
+     * these documents are opened in - rasterises an anchored DrawingML text box on import, so
+     * its characters leave the page's text layer and the line fails {@code 04_chars_missing}
+     * even though it renders in the right place.
+     *
+     * <p>So the box is kept only where the price it avoids is real. Three questions decide that,
+     * in the order that rejects the most:
+     *
+     * <ol>
+     *   <li>IS THERE ANYTHING BELOW IT TO PUSH? Only a line in the foot of the sheet
+     *       ({@link #FOOT_BAND}) is safe, because the page has already decided where that line
+     *       goes and no flow runs under it. A gap in the body of a page is white space the
+     *       page's own content has to fit around, and spending one there is measured wrong: with
+     *       every gap spent, {@code 330_cad_drawing_sheet}'s median displacement went 3.14 to
+     *       16.77 pt, {@code table-inside-two-column-page}'s worst 9.17 to 92.01, and
+     *       {@code newsletter-3col-header-footer}'s 6.26 to 28.36 (the 24 Nov census in
+     *       tools/p2w-bench/FINDINGS).</li>
+     *   <li>DOES THE LINE LAND ON THE PAPER? A gap that puts the line past the sheet's own
+     *       height is a position the paper cannot hold; it stays a box, which is the construct
+     *       that does not advance the flow past the paper.</li>
+     *   <li>CAN THIS PAGE'S BUDGET CARRY THE GAP? This is the arithmetic {@link #fitPage} does
+     *       when it gives a gap up to make a page fit (its step 1). Where the budget cannot carry
+     *       it, the gap would be clamped out of {@code w:before} anyway and the line would land
+     *       above its own baseline - the 130.6 pt footer miss on
+     *       {@code 27_two_column_left_short_txtpos} that the cap's own clamp was rejected for.
+     *       So the box is kept there too.</li>
+     * </ol>
+     *
+     * <p>Where all three hold, the gap is written as spacing-before: the line stays a flowed
+     * paragraph, Google honours {@code w:before} 1:1, the text stays in the text layer and the
+     * page is charged exactly the height the page already used. Measured in Google Docs through
+     * the real path (Drive upload -> convert -> export) on
